=== I. Sawtooth: a 2D2H2L net with a NON-ZERO fractional row is not monotone ===
weights as in fb_2D2H2L.txt except W0 row 1 = [0.5, 0.5] (hypothetical, --monotone off)
   x      key            frac     u1       u2       z(real file)  z(hypothetical)  dz/dx(hyp, a.e.)
 15.90     19216769196  0.90000  0.54310  0.54316  0.43396       2.31476          1.78379
 15.99     19325457533  0.99000  0.58863  0.58869  0.43640       2.47166          1.70250
 16.00     19337534015  0.00000  0.09369  0.09375  0.43667       0.43667          2.34319
 16.01     19349610497  0.01000  0.09875  0.09881  0.43694       0.46009          2.34091
 16.50     19941358110  0.50000  0.34662  0.34668  0.45024       1.55796          2.10107
jump at x=16: z(16^-) - z(16^+) = 2.05196
closed form sum_h v_h*(tanh(u_h + W0[1,h]) - tanh(u_h)) with u_h = 16*W0[0,h]: 2.05196
=> with a non-zero fractional row the composed map key -> z decreases at every integer of x (sawtooth);
   train_flow.py --monotone zeroes W0[1,:], which is why fb_training.json has unordered_transformed_pairs = 0

=== J. Jacobian formula check (analytic vs central finite difference), hypothetical weights, x=15.5 ===
analytic dz/dx = 2.10923217; finite difference = 2.10923217; |diff| = 1.53e-09
dz/dkey = dz/dx / var = 1.746562e-09 per key unit

=== K. Why sigma^2 = 1e16 is 'flat' ===
|z| = 1e+03: data term z^2/(2 sigma^2) = 5.000e-11; log-density offset ln sqrt(2 pi 1e16) = 19.340
|z| = 1e+06: data term z^2/(2 sigma^2) = 5.000e-05; log-density offset ln sqrt(2 pi 1e16) = 19.340
|z| = 1e+08: data term z^2/(2 sigma^2) = 5.000e-01; log-density offset ln sqrt(2 pi 1e16) = 19.340
our trainer: sigma = 1, so for z ~ 1 the data term is 0.5, of the same order as -log|dz/dx|

=== L. Batched P99 vs per-request P99 (illustration) ===
batch of 256: one 10,000 ns lookup + 255 x 50 ns = 22750 ns; reported as 22750/256 = 88.8672 ns per operation
per-request P99 of the same trace = 50 ns; max = 10000 ns

